# [[.Module]]

This PostgreSQL application was generated by GoForge. Routes, controllers,
repositories, configuration, sessions, and SQL are ordinary application-owned Go.

## Quick start

```sh
forge new issueboard --module example.com/issueboard
cd issueboard
forge make:resource Issue
forge make:job SendWelcome
forge migrate
forge test
forge build
# For plain-HTTP development, set APP_ENV=local and APP_URL=http://localhost:8080 in .env.
forge serve
```

These commands assume PostgreSQL is reachable at the
generated local database URL. If it is not already running, start the explicit
development service with `docker compose up -d` after entering the project.
GoForge never starts external services implicitly. The generated `.env`
contains unique session/outbox secrets and retains `APP_ENV=production`, which
keeps cookies HTTPS-only and rejects plaintext SMTP. For local plain-HTTP
development, change `APP_ENV` to `local` and `APP_URL` to
`http://localhost:8080` before running `forge serve` or the worker. Mailpit is
available at `http://localhost:8025`. Never use `APP_ENV=local` in a
deployed process because it disables secure cookies. Replace the local database
URL, rotate both secrets, and configure TLS-protected SMTP before deployment.
Generated Compose ports are bound to `127.0.0.1` so known development
credentials and Mailpit are not exposed on other network interfaces.
HTTP limits and timeouts are listed in `.env.example`. Enable
`APP_ENABLE_HSTS` only when production traffic always uses HTTPS, including
the trusted proxy-to-client path. `TRUSTED_PROXIES` accepts explicit,
comma-separated CIDR ranges; when it is empty, forwarding headers are ignored
and authentication throttles use the direct network peer.
`SESSION_IDLE_LIFETIME` controls inactivity expiry and
`SESSION_ABSOLUTE_LIFETIME` is a non-sliding hard deadline; the absolute value
must be at least the idle value. Keep application hosts and PostgreSQL on
synchronized clocks because both enforce session deadlines.
The default PostgreSQL session and rate-limit stores automatically share the
atomic signup transaction. Replacing either store requires supplying an
application-specific `RegistrationTransaction`; startup fails outside tests if
no cross-store transaction coordinator is configured.

Create a persistence model without an HTTP resource using either
`forge make:model Invoice` or `forge make model Invoice`. This writes ordinary
Go and paired PostgreSQL migrations, then refreshes the inspectable typed ORM.
Edit those application-owned files directly to add fields or relationships and
run `forge orm:generate` after model changes.

Create a durable typed job with `forge make:job SendWelcome` (or
`forge make job SendWelcome`). Edit its ordinary payload and handler under
`internal/jobs`, then dispatch it through the generated typed helper. A
dispatcher can use either `*sql.DB` or `dispatcher.Using(tx)`, so enqueueing can
share the transaction that commits a domain write. Run workers with
`forge queue:work`; inspect terminal failures with `forge queue:failed`, and use
`forge queue:retry <id>` (or `--all`) or `forge queue:forget <id>`. Jobs are delivered at
least once, so handlers must make external effects idempotent. Build the
production worker with `go build -trimpath -o bin/worker ./cmd/worker`.
`JOB_CANCELLATION_TIMEOUT` bounds how long a handler may ignore cancellation;
after that deadline the worker stops heartbeating and exits so another worker
can reclaim the fenced lease.

Password recovery is ready at `/forgot-password` and
`POST /auth/password/forgot`; reset links finish at `/reset-password` or
`POST /auth/password/reset`. Start `go run ./cmd/worker` (or `forge queue:work`)
to deliver them. Links always use the validated `APP_URL`, never request Host or
forwarding headers. Only a selector and SHA-256 digest are stored for the
single-use token. Recipient, subject, text, HTML, and the complete link live in
AES-256-GCM outbox ciphertext, while the durable job payload contains only the
opaque outbox ID. `MAIL_OUTBOX_KEY` must be an independent 32-byte unpadded
base64url secret; rotating it makes pending messages unreadable, so drain or
deliberately discard them first. Production SMTP requires `starttls` or
`implicit` TLS. `none` is accepted only for unauthenticated loopback SMTP in
`APP_ENV=local`. SMTP delivery is at least once: a connection failure around
the provider acceptance boundary can duplicate a message, but reset links
remain single-use.
Every envelope also receives an application-owned delayed cleanup job. Accepted
delivery removes it immediately; otherwise encrypted data is retained for at
most the explicit seven-day `internal/jobs.MailOutboxRetention` window. Edit
that ordinary Go policy when the application requires a different retention
period.

Create an application-owned dual-template message with
`forge make:mail AuditNotice`. The generated Go under `internal/mail`, Forge
HTML under `resources/views/mail`, and standard-library text template are the
complete implementation and can be edited or replaced directly.

Views live in `resources/views` and compile to the inspectable
`resources/views/views_gen.go` artifact consumed in production. Generate an
anonymous component with `forge make:component Notice`, then edit its declared
props and slots directly. Register application helpers once in
`resources/views/viewfuncs/functions.go`; the project compiler and production renderer
use the same function map. Run `forge views:compile` after source changes and
use `forge views:compile --check` in CI. `forge serve` compiles views before
starting the application and recompiles them after stable source changes.

Run the opinionated project checks and build with:

```sh
forge test
forge build
```

Both commands first check the generated ORM and then the compiled views without
rewriting either artifact. `forge test` then runs exactly `go test ./...`.
`forge build` stages a trimmed server executable and publishes it only after a
successful compile as `bin/app` on Unix or `bin/app.exe` on Windows. A failed or
cancelled build preserves the last-good binary.

Use the exact underlying workflow whenever the default is not suitable:

```sh
forge orm:generate --check
forge views:compile --check
go test ./...
go build -trimpath -o bin/app ./cmd/server
go run ./cmd/server
```

Direct Go commands remain available for custom packages, flags, tags, targets,
and output paths. `forge test` and `forge build` do not start PostgreSQL or
Compose, apply migrations, select a test database, or change `.env`. `forge
serve` watches Go, Forge view, SQL, module, environment, and manifest inputs. It
builds candidates outside the repository, proves `/health` on a private
loopback address, and promotes only a healthy replacement behind one stable
public address. Invalid source leaves the last-good server reachable and
recovers after correction. Refresh the browser after a successful reload; the
proxy does not inject scripts or rewrite responses. Frontend HMR and
multi-process development orchestration remain separate future work. Candidate
promotion proves `/health` liveness, while `/ready` continues to reflect the
database and exact migration state; run `forge migrate` explicitly after SQL
changes.

Listener ownership needs no extra tool on Linux or Windows. macOS and other
supported Unix targets use the system `lsof`; use `go run ./cmd/server`
directly when it is unavailable.

Send `Content-Type: application/json` with JSON request bodies. Other media
types receive 415 so browser forms cannot submit JSON authentication requests.
Register a user with `POST /auth/register`, then use the returned session cookie
with the authenticated resource endpoints. `PUT /auth/password` changes the
password while rotating the caller and revoking older sessions;
`POST /auth/logout-all` signs out every session. The browser equivalents live
at `/settings/security` and are CSRF protected. Successful login upgrades an
outdated password hash without revoking sessions. Run `go test ./...` and build a
custom production binary with `go build -trimpath -o bin/app ./cmd/server`, or
use the opinionated `forge test` and `forge build` workflow described above.

Inspect command implementations in `cmd/server`, `cmd/worker`, `cmd/console`, and `cmd/views`,
the complete human-owned route table in `routes/routes.go`, and generated
resource wiring in `routes/resources_gen.go`.
